1
00:00:02,350 --> 00:00:05,318
A channel grant is the single most
important message on a trunked system: the

2
00:00:05,318 --> 00:00:08,287
moment it is broadcast, a call is
starting, and the message says exactly

3
00:00:08,287 --> 00:00:11,255
where.

4
00:00:11,605 --> 00:00:14,154
Everything trunking promises — many
groups, few channels — comes down to this

5
00:00:14,154 --> 00:00:16,702
one transaction.

6
00:00:16,822 --> 00:00:17,762
A user keys up.

7
00:00:17,882 --> 00:00:20,831
Their radio sends a call request on the
control channel.

8
00:00:20,951 --> 00:00:24,349
The site controller picks a free voice
channel from the pool, packs an answer

9
00:00:24,349 --> 00:00:27,747
into a short signalling block, and
broadcasts it: talkgroup 101, go to

10
00:00:27,747 --> 00:00:31,145
channel 3.

11
00:00:31,495 --> 00:00:34,851
Every radio affiliated to talkgroup 101
hears that grant and retunes together, in

12
00:00:34,851 --> 00:00:38,206
a fraction of a second.

13
00:00:38,325 --> 00:00:40,717
So does every monitor following the
system.

14
00:00:40,837 --> 00:00:43,786
One message, and an entire group — plus
everyone listening in — moves to the same

15
00:00:43,786 --> 00:00:46,735
place at the same instant.

16
00:00:47,085 --> 00:00:50,057
Look inside the message and there are
three essentials.

17
00:00:50,177 --> 00:00:53,224
First, the target: a talkgroup for a group
call, or a source-and-destination pair for

18
00:00:53,224 --> 00:00:56,272
a private call.

19
00:00:56,392 --> 00:00:59,991
Second, the channel — usually not a raw
frequency, but a channel number the radio

20
00:00:59,991 --> 00:01:03,590
maps to a frequency through the system's
channel plan.

21
00:01:03,710 --> 00:01:06,844
And third, on TDMA systems, a timeslot,
because two calls can share one frequency

22
00:01:06,844 --> 00:01:09,979
in time.

23
00:01:10,330 --> 00:01:13,616
A grant is broadcast once — but a radio
might miss it.

24
00:01:13,735 --> 00:01:17,044
It was out of range, powered off, or busy
on another channel.

25
00:01:17,164 --> 00:01:19,864
So while the call runs, the control
channel repeats a grant update announcing

26
00:01:19,864 --> 00:01:22,563
the call in progress.

27
00:01:22,683 --> 00:01:25,713
A late arrival reads the update and joins
mid-call.

28
00:01:25,833 --> 00:01:28,312
That is late entry, and it is why trunked
systems feel seamless even as radios come

29
00:01:28,312 --> 00:01:30,790
and go.

30
00:01:31,140 --> 00:01:34,736
The flavors mirror the traffic: group
voice grants for the everyday case,

31
00:01:34,736 --> 00:01:38,332
unit-to-unit grants for private calls
between two radio IDs, grant updates for

32
00:01:38,332 --> 00:01:41,929
late entry, and data grants that assign a
channel for a short packet transfer

33
00:01:41,929 --> 00:01:45,525
instead of voice.

34
00:01:45,875 --> 00:01:48,308
One more wrinkle: a grant is a promise of
a free channel, and sometimes there isn't

35
00:01:48,308 --> 00:01:50,740
one.

36
00:01:50,860 --> 00:01:53,954
During a busy incident the controller may
queue the request instead, and the grant

37
00:01:53,954 --> 00:01:57,048
arrives seconds later when a channel frees
up.

38
00:01:57,168 --> 00:01:58,933
Grade of service, made audible.

39
00:01:59,283 --> 00:02:03,091
GopherTrunk's trunk-following engine is,
at heart, a grant reader.

40
00:02:03,211 --> 00:02:06,735
Every grant on the control channel is
parsed in real time; the channel number

41
00:02:06,735 --> 00:02:10,258
runs through the decoded channel plan to
become a frequency; and a receiver is

42
00:02:10,258 --> 00:02:13,782
tasked at that frequency — and that
timeslot — before the first syllable of

43
00:02:13,782 --> 00:02:17,306
audio arrives.

44
00:02:17,425 --> 00:02:19,886
Repeat for every grant, and one wideband
capture reconstructs the whole system's

45
00:02:19,886 --> 00:02:22,348
traffic.

46
00:02:23,498 --> 00:02:26,116
So: a channel grant is the control-channel
message that assigns a call to a voice

47
00:02:26,116 --> 00:02:28,734
channel.

48
00:02:28,854 --> 00:02:32,256
It names the target, the channel, and on
TDMA the slot — and its updates repeat

49
00:02:32,256 --> 00:02:35,657
mid-call so latecomers can join.

50
00:02:35,777 --> 00:02:37,402
Full write-up linked below.
